questions.text:
Let  [latex]X [/latex] and  [latex]Y [/latex] be continuous random variables with probability density functions [latex]\mathrm{P}_{\mathrm{X}}(x)[/latex] and [latex]\mathrm{P}_{\mathrm{Y}}(y)[/latex], respectively. Further, let [latex]\mathrm{Y}=\mathrm{X}^{2}[/latex] and [latex]
\mathrm{P}_{\mathrm{X}}(x)= \begin{cases}1, & x \in (0,1] \\ 0, & \text { otherwise }\end{cases}
[/latex] Which one of the following options is correct?

Consider the state-space model [latex]
\begin{aligned}
\dot{x}({t}) & ={Ax}({t})+{Br}({t}) \\
{y}({t}) & ={Cx}({t})
\end{aligned}
[/latex] Where [latex]{x}({t}), {r}({t}), {y}({t})[/latex] are the state, input and output, respectively. The matrices [latex]{A}, {B}, {C}[/latex] are given below [latex]
A=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & 1 \\
-2 & -3
\end{array}\right], {B}=\left[\begin{array}{l}
0 \\
1
\end{array}\right], {C}=\left[\begin{array}{ll}
1 & 0
\end{array}\right]
[/latex] The sum of the magnitudes of the poles is ________ (round off to nearest integer).
